2026-01-08 01:55:31 | INFO | Q695 id=BV1114y1X72X_00-00-44_00-01-06 | baseline_pred=B | gold=B | prompt_variant=strong | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=True | raw=The question is about whether the same pitch difference will be perceived from 200 Hz to 400 Hz and from 400 Hz to 600 Hz. \n\n1. **Understanding the relationship**: Frequency and pitch have an exponential relationship. This means that equal frequency intervals do not correspond to equal perceived pitch intervals.\n  \n2. **Analyzing the intervals**:\n   - From 200 Hz to 400 Hz: This is a doubling of the frequency (200 Hz to 400 Hz).\n   - From 400 Hz to 600 Hz: This is a 200 Hz increase, but not a doubling of the frequency.\n\n3. **Perception of pitch**: Since the perception of pitch is related to the logarithmic scale of frequency, a doubling of frequency (like 200 Hz to 400 Hz) is perceived as a more significant pitch change than a 200 Hz increase at higher frequencies (like 400 Hz to 600 Hz).\n\nGiven this reasoning, the perceived pitch difference will not be the same for the two intervals. \n\nAnswer: \boxed{B}
